<!DOCTYPE template>
<skin>
  <manifest>
    <title>TriMixxx</title>
    <author>Sam</author>
    <version>1.0</version>
    <description>Single-deck CDJ-style skin for 1024x600 touchscreen</description>
    <attributes>
      <attribute config_key="[Master],num_decks">1</attribute>
      <attribute config_key="[Master],num_samplers">0</attribute>
      <attribute config_key="[Master],num_preview_decks">0</attribute>
      <attribute config_key="[EffectRack1],num_effectunits">0</attribute>
      <!-- 2 = RGB waveform -->
      <attribute config_key="[Waveform],WaveformType">2</attribute>
      <!-- Default to deck view, not library -->
      <attribute persist="true" config_key="[Master],show_library">0</attribute>
      <!-- Shutdown menu. Mixxx itself cannot touch the OS, so POWER only sets
           skin-local state: shutdown_confirm raises the overlay, and YES pulses
           shutdown_now, which the PiMidiDaemon mapping turns into a SysEx the
           pi-midi-daemon service acts on. Not persisted, so a boot can never
           come up mid-confirm, let alone mid-shutdown. -->
      <attribute persist="false" config_key="[TriMixxx],shutdown_confirm">0</attribute>
      <attribute persist="false" config_key="[TriMixxx],shutdown_now">0</attribute>
      <!-- Where the tempo fader physically is, in the same -1..1 the deck's own
           `rate` uses, so the tempo panel can show what the fader is ASKING for
           while soft-takeover is still holding it off. Without it there is
           nothing on screen to aim with: the fader is disconnected, the number
           does not move, and catching the tempo is a blind hunt.

           -2 means "the fader has not been heard from since boot", which is not
           the same as centre and must not be drawn as one. Not persisted: a
           stale position from last boot would be a lie about a physical
           control, which is the one thing this must never be. -->
      <attribute persist="false" config_key="[TriMixxx],tempo_fader">-2</attribute>
    </attributes>
  </manifest>

  <Style src="skin:/style.qss"/>

  <!-- The <Layout> and <Children> below are what make this a ">=1.12.0 style"
       skin, and that is LOAD-BEARING for the Qt menu bar, not tidiness.

       LegacySkinParser::parseNode() decides which of two root paths to take by
       whether the <skin> node has a <Layout> (legacyskinparser.cpp). Without
       one it takes the legacy path, which applies <Style> to an INNER widget,
       wraps that in an outer widget, and returns the OUTER one as the skin
       root. MixxxMainWindow then does

           m_pMenuBar->setStyleSheet(m_pCentralWidget->styleSheet())

       against that outer widget, whose stylesheet is an empty string, so the
       menu bar got nothing and kept its stock light palette. The rest of the
       skin still looked right because the inner widget's sheet propagates down
       to its own children. The menu bar is the one widget that is NOT one of
       those, because it belongs to MixxxMainWindow rather than to this file.

       With a <Layout>, parseWidgetGroup() handles the root, applies <Style> to
       the very widget it returns, and the menu bar gets the real sheet.

       This also silences the two "Invalid node name in skin" warnings the log
       carried for <manifest> and <Style>: the legacy path walked every child of
       <skin> as if it were a widget, while parseWidgetGroup only descends into
       <Children>.

       One thing given up: the legacy path centred the skin in an outer widget
       for fullscreen. The panel is exactly 1024x600 and so is this skin, so
       there is nothing to centre. On a LARGER display it would now sit top-left
       rather than centred. -->
  <Layout>vertical</Layout>
  <Children>

  <!-- Stacked so the shutdown confirm can sit ON TOP of the whole UI. Mixxx's
       stacked layout is a QStackedLayout in StackAll mode, so every child shows
       at once (rather than one-at-a-time) and a child's `visible` connection is
       still honoured, which is what makes the overlay possible in pure XML.

       ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING AND BACKWARDS: the FIRST child renders on TOP.
       Mixxx inserts a zero-sized dummy first (so the stacked layout force-shows
       that instead of clobbering a real child's `visible` connection), which
       means QStackedLayout::insertWidget takes its `else` branch for every real
       child and calls widget->lower() on it, so each one lands UNDER the
       previous. With ShutdownOverlay listed last it rendered behind the opaque
       #Root and was invisible even while shown. Stock skins rely on the same
       rule: LateNight's button_1state_optional.xml lists the cover Label first
       and the button it covers second. -->
  <WidgetGroup>
    <ObjectName>RootStack</ObjectName>
    <Layout>stacked</Layout>
    <MinimumSize>1024,600</MinimumSize>
    <MaximumSize>1024,600</MaximumSize>
    <Children>

      <!-- Media notifications. FIRST child on purpose: RootStack renders in
           reverse (see the note above), so this is the only position that puts
           it over both the deck and the browser. It is transparent to the
           mouse, so covering the panel costs nothing. -->
      <DeckToast>
        <ObjectName>DeckToast</ObjectName>
        <MinimumSize>1024,600</MinimumSize>
        <MaximumSize>1024,600</MaximumSize>
      </DeckToast>

      <!-- Shutdown confirm. Covers everything, so the deck is unreachable while
           it is up; visible only while shutdown_confirm = 1. Mixxx cannot power
           the Pi off itself: SHUT DOWN pulses shutdown_now, the PiMidiDaemon
           mapping turns that into SysEx F0 7D 01 F7, and the pi-midi-daemon
           service runs systemctl poweroff. -->
      <WidgetGroup>
        <ObjectName>ShutdownOverlay</ObjectName>
        <Layout>vertical</Layout>
        <MinimumSize>1024,600</MinimumSize>
        <MaximumSize>1024,600</MaximumSize>
        <Connection>
          <ConfigKey persist="false">[TriMixxx],shutdown_confirm</ConfigKey>
          <BindProperty>visible</BindProperty>
        </Connection>
        <Children>
          <WidgetGroup><SizePolicy>e,e</SizePolicy><Children/></WidgetGroup>

          <WidgetGroup>
            <ObjectName>ShutdownDialog</ObjectName>
            <Layout>vertical</Layout>
            <MinimumSize>1024,270</MinimumSize>
            <MaximumSize>1024,270</MaximumSize>
            <Children>
              <Label>
                <ObjectName>ShutdownTitle</ObjectName>
                <MinimumSize>1024,80</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>1024,80</MaximumSize>
                <Text>Shut down the deck?</Text>
              </Label>
              <Label>
                <ObjectName>ShutdownHint</ObjectName>
                <MinimumSize>1024,40</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>1024,40</MaximumSize>
                <Text>Wait for the screen to go dark before cutting power.</Text>
              </Label>
              <WidgetGroup>
                <Layout>horizontal</Layout>
                <MinimumSize>1024,140</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>1024,140</MaximumSize>
                <Children>
                  <WidgetGroup><SizePolicy>e,f</SizePolicy><Children/></WidgetGroup>

                  <!-- CANCEL is momentary: the RELEASE writes 0 to
                       shutdown_confirm, which hides this overlay.

                       LeftClickIsPushButton is required, not decoration. A
                       skin-created control is a ControlPushButton in TOGGLE
                       mode, and WPushButton copies that mode off the control,
                       which makes the connection EMIT_ON_PRESS only, so
                       setControlParameterUp() drops the release. The press
                       alone writes 1 to a control already at 1, i.e. nothing
                       happens and the overlay can never be dismissed. Forcing
                       PUSH restores EMIT_ON_PRESS_AND_RELEASE. -->
                  <PushButton>
                    <ObjectName>ShutdownNo</ObjectName>
                    <MinimumSize>220,110</MinimumSize>
                    <MaximumSize>220,110</MaximumSize>
                    <NumberStates>1</NumberStates>
                    <LeftClickIsPushButton>true</LeftClickIsPushButton>
                    <State><Number>0</Number><Text>CANCEL</Text></State>
                    <Connection>
                      <ConfigKey persist="false">[TriMixxx],shutdown_confirm</ConfigKey>
                      <ButtonState>LeftButton</ButtonState>
                    </Connection>
                  </PushButton>

                  <WidgetGroup><MinimumSize>60,10</MinimumSize><MaximumSize>60,10</MaximumSize><Children/></WidgetGroup>

                  <!-- SHUT DOWN pulses shutdown_now (1 on press, 0 on release);
                       the mapping fires on the rising edge. The overlay stays up
                       afterwards on purpose: the deck is on its way down, and
                       if nothing happens CANCEL still gets you out. -->
                  <PushButton>
                    <ObjectName>ShutdownYes</ObjectName>
                    <MinimumSize>220,110</MinimumSize>
                    <MaximumSize>220,110</MaximumSize>
                    <NumberStates>1</NumberStates>
                    <!-- Same reason as CANCEL, plus one of its own: without the
                         release, shutdown_now would latch at 1 forever, so a
                         second press would write 1 to a control already at 1,
                         raise no valueChanged, and never fire again. -->
                    <LeftClickIsPushButton>true</LeftClickIsPushButton>
                    <State><Number>0</Number><Text>SHUT DOWN</Text></State>
                    <Connection>
                      <ConfigKey persist="false">[TriMixxx],shutdown_now</ConfigKey>
                      <ButtonState>LeftButton</ButtonState>
                    </Connection>
                  </PushButton>

                  <WidgetGroup><SizePolicy>e,f</SizePolicy><Children/></WidgetGroup>
                </Children>
              </WidgetGroup>
            </Children>
          </WidgetGroup>

          <WidgetGroup><SizePolicy>e,e</SizePolicy><Children/></WidgetGroup>
        </Children>
      </WidgetGroup>

      <WidgetGroup>
        <ObjectName>Root</ObjectName>
        <Layout>vertical</Layout>
        <MinimumSize>1024,600</MinimumSize>
        <MaximumSize>1024,600</MaximumSize>
        <Children>

          <!-- The panel is recessed behind a lip on every edge, and the top
               one eats the first rows of whatever is drawn there. The browser
               insets itself; this is the same strip for the deck view.

               Hidden while browsing, on the same condition as the header below
               it, because the browser already has its own and two would stack
               to seventy-two pixels of nothing. -->
          <WidgetGroup>
            <ObjectName>DeckBezelPad</ObjectName>
            <Layout>horizontal</Layout>
            <MinimumSize>1024,36</MinimumSize>
            <MaximumSize>1024,36</MaximumSize>
            <Connection>
              <ConfigKey persist="true">[Master],show_library</ConfigKey>
              <Transform><Not/></Transform>
              <BindProperty>visible</BindProperty>
            </Connection>
            <Children/>
          </WidgetGroup>

          <!-- =========== TOP HEADER (always visible) =========== -->
          <WidgetGroup>
            <ObjectName>TopHeader</ObjectName>
            <Layout>horizontal</Layout>
            <MinimumSize>1024,80</MinimumSize>
            <MaximumSize>1024,80</MaximumSize>
            <!-- Gone while browsing. A standalone deck has one screen, and a
                 track list is worth more than a header repeating what the
                 waveform already says. -->
            <Connection>
              <ConfigKey persist="true">[Master],show_library</ConfigKey>
              <Transform><Not/></Transform>
              <BindProperty>visible</BindProperty>
            </Connection>
            <Children>

              <!-- Cover art -->
              <CoverArt>
                <Group>[Channel1]</Group>
                <MinimumSize>72,72</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>72,72</MaximumSize>
                <DefaultCover>cover_default.svg</DefaultCover>
              </CoverArt>

              <!-- Title / Artist, beside the cover art.
                   The explicit sizes are what make this render at all: with
                   none the TrackPropertys have no height, and this group is
                   vertically Fixed, so the column collapsed to 0px and was
                   simply never drawn. Stock skins always size these
                   (e.g. <Size>0e,40f</Size>). -->
              <WidgetGroup>
                <ObjectName>TitleArtistColumn</ObjectName>
                <Layout>vertical</Layout>
                <!-- A third of the panel and no more. Track titles run long and
                     this one used to expand, squeezing the phase meter down to
                     whatever was left over. -->
                <MinimumSize>200,72</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>341,72</MaximumSize>
                <SizePolicy>me,f</SizePolicy>
                <Children>
                  <TrackProperty>
                    <ObjectName>TitleText</ObjectName>
                    <Group>[Channel1]</Group>
                    <Property>title</Property>
                    <Size>0me,42f</Size>
                    <Elide>right</Elide>
                  </TrackProperty>
                  <!-- The key, then the artist. In that order because the
                       artist elides and the key must not: it is three
                       characters that decide whether the next track fits, and
                       a long title pushing them off the row would be exactly
                       the wrong thing to lose. -->
                  <WidgetGroup>
                    <ObjectName>ArtistRow</ObjectName>
                    <Layout>horizontal</Layout>
                    <Size>0me,28f</Size>
                    <Children>
                      <!-- Camelot, always, whatever the preferences say: the
                           row is sized for three characters and "G# minor" is
                           eight. `Lancelot` is Mixxx's name for the Camelot
                           wheel: the same 1A..12B, spelled after the other of
                           the two products that popularised it.

                           Bound to `key` and not `file_key`: with KEY SYNC
                           engaged the deck is not playing in the key the file
                           was written in, and the number worth reading off a
                           deck is the one coming out of it. -->
                      <!-- The SAME key, twice, one lime and one purple, and
                           exactly one of them visible: purple while KEY SYNC
                           is holding this deck at that key, lime otherwise.

                           Two widgets rather than one, because a colour cannot
                           be driven from a control. Qt stylesheets can select
                           on a widget property, but BindProperty can only
                           write properties the widget's metaobject already
                           declares (see propertyFromWidget in
                           controlwidgetconnection.cpp), so a made-up
                           `keySync` property is not written at all: it logs
                           one warning per update and nothing changes colour.
                           `visible` is a real QWidget property, and a hidden
                           widget takes no space in a QHBoxLayout, so the two
                           never fight over the row.

                           Camelot, always, whatever the preferences say: the
                           row is sized for three characters and "G# minor" is
                           eight. `Lancelot` is Mixxx's name for the Camelot
                           wheel: the same 1A..12B, spelled after the other of
                           the two products that popularised it.

                           Bound to `key` and not `file_key`: with KEY SYNC
                           engaged the deck is not playing in the key the file
                           was written in, and the number worth reading off a
                           deck is the one coming out of it. -->
                      <Key>
                        <ObjectName>KeyBadge</ObjectName>
                        <Group>[Channel1]</Group>
                        <!-- Fixed, not a minimum: without the `f` the width
                             is only a floor and the label grows into whatever
                             the artist beside it is not using, so the artist
                             starts at a different place on every track. 46 px
                             is three characters of the 16 px face plus the
                             title's own left padding. -->
                        <Size>46f,28f</Size>
                        <Notation>Lancelot</Notation>
                        <DisplayCents>false</DisplayCents>
                        <Connection>
                          <ConfigKey>[Channel1],key</ConfigKey>
                        </Connection>
                        <Connection>
                          <ConfigKey>[ProLink],key_sync_enabled</ConfigKey>
                          <Transform><Not/></Transform>
                          <BindProperty>visible</BindProperty>
                        </Connection>
                      </Key>
                      <Key>
                        <ObjectName>KeyBadgeSynced</ObjectName>
                        <Group>[Channel1]</Group>
                        <Size>46f,28f</Size>
                        <Notation>Lancelot</Notation>
                        <DisplayCents>false</DisplayCents>
                        <Connection>
                          <ConfigKey>[Channel1],key</ConfigKey>
                        </Connection>
                        <Connection>
                          <ConfigKey>[ProLink],key_sync_enabled</ConfigKey>
                          <BindProperty>visible</BindProperty>
                        </Connection>
                      </Key>

                      <TrackProperty>
                        <ObjectName>ArtistText</ObjectName>
                        <Group>[Channel1]</Group>
                        <Property>artist</Property>
                        <Size>0me,28f</Size>
                        <Elide>right</Elide>
                      </TrackProperty>
                    </Children>
                  </WidgetGroup>
                </Children>
              </WidgetGroup>

              <!-- Pro DJ Link phase meter: where the network's tempo master is
                   in its bar, over where this deck is in its own. Unlabelled
                   apart from the master's player number overlaid on the top
                   row; in the header there is no width for anything else, and
                   the bottom row is always this deck. -->
              <ProLinkPhaseMeter>
                <ObjectName>ProLinkPhase</ObjectName>
                <Group>[Channel1]</Group>
                <!-- Expanding: it gets the whole gap between the title and
                     the clock, which is the widest the two rows can be and so
                     the finest the offset between them can be read. -->
                <MinimumSize>200,56</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>-1,56</MaximumSize>
                <SizePolicy>me,f</SizePolicy>
                <MasterColor>#ff6600</MasterColor>
                <DeckColor>#44ccff</DeckColor>
              </ProLinkPhaseMeter>

              <!-- The BPM/KEY column stood here. The tempo went to the
                   seven-segment face over the waveform, where a CDJ puts it,
                   and the key went beside the artist, where it sits under the
                   title it belongs to rather than in a column of its own.
                   Its 120 px go to the phase meter, which is the one widget
                   here that is worth more the wider it is.

                   The Key widget it held never drew anything: it carried a
                   DisplayType, which WKey has no such thing as, and no
                   Connection at all, so it was bound to nothing and rendered
                   an empty string for as long as it existed. -->

              <!-- SYNC and MASTER, the two Pro DJ Link buttons a CDJ has.
                   Beside the clock rather than beside the phase meter they
                   act on, because the meter is deliberately allowed to expand
                   into whatever width is spare and anything to its right would
                   be pushed around as track titles change length. -->
              <WidgetGroup>
                <ObjectName>ProLinkButtons</ObjectName>
                <Layout>horizontal</Layout>
                <MinimumSize>116,72</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>116,72</MaximumSize>
                <Children>
                  <PushButton>
                    <ObjectName>ProLinkSync</ObjectName>
                    <Size>52,52f</Size>
                    <NumberStates>2</NumberStates>
                    <State>
                      <Number>0</Number>
                      <Unpressed scalemode="STRETCH_ASPECT">sync_off.svg</Unpressed>
                      <Pressed scalemode="STRETCH_ASPECT">sync_on.svg</Pressed>
                    </State>
                    <State>
                      <Number>1</Number>
                      <Unpressed scalemode="STRETCH_ASPECT">sync_on.svg</Unpressed>
                      <Pressed scalemode="STRETCH_ASPECT">sync_off.svg</Pressed>
                    </State>
                    <Connection>
                      <ConfigKey>[ProLink],sync_enabled</ConfigKey>
                      <ButtonState>LeftButton</ButtonState>
                    </Connection>
                  </PushButton>

                  <!-- Two controls, on purpose. The button LIGHTS from
                       `is_master`, which is what the network settled, and it
                       CLICKS into `take_master`, which is only a request: the
                       deck that holds mastership has to hand it over first, and
                       that takes a packet or two. Lighting it from the click
                       would show us as master before we were, which is the one
                       thing a master indicator must never do. -->
                  <PushButton>
                    <ObjectName>ProLinkMaster</ObjectName>
                    <Size>52,52f</Size>
                    <NumberStates>2</NumberStates>
                    <State>
                      <Number>0</Number>
                      <Unpressed scalemode="STRETCH_ASPECT">master_off.svg</Unpressed>
                      <Pressed scalemode="STRETCH_ASPECT">master_on.svg</Pressed>
                    </State>
                    <State>
                      <Number>1</Number>
                      <Unpressed scalemode="STRETCH_ASPECT">master_on.svg</Unpressed>
                      <Pressed scalemode="STRETCH_ASPECT">master_off.svg</Pressed>
                    </State>
                    <Connection>
                      <ConfigKey>[ProLink],is_master</ConfigKey>
                      <ConnectValueFromWidget>false</ConnectValueFromWidget>
                    </Connection>
                    <Connection>
                      <ConfigKey>[ProLink],take_master</ConfigKey>
                      <ButtonState>LeftButton</ButtonState>
                      <ConnectValueToWidget>false</ConnectValueToWidget>
                    </Connection>
                  </PushButton>
                </Children>
              </WidgetGroup>

              <!-- TIME column -->
              <WidgetGroup>
                <ObjectName>TimeColumn</ObjectName>
                <Layout>vertical</Layout>
                <!-- Narrow on purpose. The mode renders elapsed and remaining
                     together in ONE widget ; there is no way to split them,
                     since every WNumberPos reads the same global
                     [Controls],ShowDurationRemaining ; so the way to stack
                     them is to give the label less width than the combined
                     string needs and let it wrap. -->
                <MinimumSize>132,72</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>132,72</MaximumSize>
                <Children>
                  <!-- ONE widget, showing elapsed AND remaining together.
                       There is no <ShowRemaining> element: every WNumberPos
                       reads the one global control
                       [Controls],ShowDurationRemaining, so a second widget
                       could only ever repeat the first, which is exactly what
                       it did. mixxx.cfg sets PositionDisplay 2
                       (TrackTime::DisplayMode::ELAPSED_AND_REMAINING), which is
                       what makes this show both. -->
                  <NumberPos>
                    <ObjectName>TimeElapsed</ObjectName>
                    <Group>[Channel1]</Group>
                    <!-- Explicitly the whole column. Without a Size the label
                         takes its hint, which is one line high, and the second
                         line of the wrapped string is simply cut off. -->
                    <Size>132,72f</Size>
                    <Connection>
                      <ConfigKey>[Channel1],playposition</ConfigKey>
                    </Connection>
                  </NumberPos>
                </Children>
              </WidgetGroup>

            </Children>
          </WidgetGroup>

          <!-- =========== MIDDLE: DECK VIEW (shown when library hidden) =========== -->
          <WidgetGroup>
            <ObjectName>DeckView</ObjectName>
            <Layout>vertical</Layout>
            <!-- 600 total - 80 header. There is no bottom bar any more, so the
                 deck owns everything under the header. -->
            <MinimumSize>1024,520</MinimumSize>
            <MaximumSize>1024,520</MaximumSize>
            <Connection>
              <ConfigKey persist="true">[Master],show_library</ConfigKey>
              <Transform><Not/></Transform>
              <BindProperty>visible</BindProperty>
            </Connection>
            <Children>

              <!-- The FX strip and the waveform, side by side. The strip is
                   the rack's master where a DJ is actually looking during a
                   transition (effects-prd.md 16); the waveform gives up 76 px
                   for it. -->
              <WidgetGroup>
                <ObjectName>WaveformRow</ObjectName>
                <Layout>horizontal</Layout>
                <MinimumSize>1024,414</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>1024,414</MaximumSize>
                <Children>

              <DeckFxStrip>
                <ObjectName>DeckFxStrip</ObjectName>
                <MinimumSize>76,414</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>76,414</MaximumSize>
              </DeckFxStrip>

              <!-- The waveform, with the tempo read-out laid over it.
                   Stacked, not side by side: QStackedLayout in StackAll mode
                   draws every child with later ones on top, so the numbers
                   float over the waveform instead of stealing width from it. -->
              <WidgetGroup>
                <ObjectName>WaveformStack</ObjectName>
                <Layout>stacked</Layout>
                <MinimumSize>948,414</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>948,414</MaximumSize>
                <Children>
              <!-- Scrolling waveform -->
              <Visual>
                <ObjectName>ScrollingWaveform</ObjectName>
                <Group>[Channel1]</Group>
                <!-- 360 + 58 (phase meter) + 76 (bottom row) = 494. Not 520: the
                     window's menu bar takes about 22 px off the top that the old
                     budget never counted, and the last row in the column is the
                     one that gets squeezed for it. The waveform
                     absorbs every row of buttons that used to sit under it,
                     rather than leaving a hole. -->
                <MinimumSize>948,414</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>948,414</MaximumSize>
                <SignalHighColor>#ff5544</SignalHighColor>
                <SignalMidColor>#88ff00</SignalMidColor>
                <SignalLowColor>#2266ff</SignalLowColor>
                <PlayPosColor>#ffffff</PlayPosColor>
                <BgColor>#000000</BgColor>
                <AxesColor>#444444</AxesColor>
                <BeatColor>#cccccc</BeatColor>
                <!-- First beat of each bar, Rekordbox-style. Anchored on the
                     intro cue, so a track with no intro cue shows plain beats. -->
                <DownbeatColor>#ff0000</DownbeatColor>
                <EndOfTrackColor>#ff2200</EndOfTrackColor>
                <!-- Hotcues: one auto-mark per set hotcue (colored by the hotcue's
                     own color); %1 = hotcue number. -->
                <DefaultMark>
                  <Align>bottom|right</Align>
                  <Color>#88ff00</Color>
                  <TextColor>#0c0c0c</TextColor>
                  <Text> %1 </Text>
                </DefaultMark>
                <!-- Cue point (red, distinct from the orange loop) -->
                <Mark>
                  <Control>cue_point</Control>
                  <Text>CUE</Text>
                  <Align>top|right</Align>
                  <Color>#ff0033</Color>
                  <TextColor>#ffffff</TextColor>
                </Mark>
                <!-- Active loop: an orange window between loop in/out.
                     Bright while looping (loop_enabled=1); stays as a dimmer amber
                     once you exit the loop (the in/out positions persist), and goes
                     bright again on reloop. Cleared only by loop_remove. -->
                <MarkRange>
                  <StartControl>loop_start_position</StartControl>
                  <EndControl>loop_end_position</EndControl>
                  <EnabledControl>loop_enabled</EnabledControl>
                  <Color>#ff8800</Color>
                  <Opacity>0.75</Opacity>
                  <DisabledColor>#b35f00</DisabledColor>
                  <DisabledOpacity>0.4</DisabledOpacity>
                </MarkRange>
                <Mark>
                  <Control>loop_start_position</Control>
                  <Text>&#8635;</Text>
                  <Align>top|left</Align>
                  <Color>#ff8800</Color>
                  <TextColor>#0c0c0c</TextColor>
                </Mark>
                <Mark>
                  <Control>loop_end_position</Control>
                  <Align>top|right</Align>
                  <Color>#ff8800</Color>
                  <TextColor>#0c0c0c</TextColor>
                </Mark>
              </Visual>

                  <!-- Tempo, pitch and range, painted over the waveform by
                       one widget. Not a group of labels: Mixxx's stacked layout
                       forces Qt::AlignCenter onto every layout it builds,
                       nested ones included, so a group inside it sizes to its
                       hint instead of filling and collapses to a sliver. -->
                  <TempoPanel>
                    <ObjectName>TempoPanel</ObjectName>
                    <Group>[Channel1]</Group>
                    <MinimumSize>948,414</MinimumSize>
                    <MaximumSize>948,414</MaximumSize>
                    <TempoFont>Digital-7 Mono</TempoFont>
                    <TempoSize>52</TempoSize>
                    <TempoColor>#ff6600</TempoColor>
                  </TempoPanel>

                </Children>
              </WidgetGroup>

                </Children>
              </WidgetGroup>

              <!-- The minimap. Taller than it was, and with a dead strip
                   below it for the bezel. -->
              <WidgetGroup>
                <ObjectName>OverviewRow</ObjectName>
                <Layout>horizontal</Layout>
                <MinimumSize>1024,72</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>1024,72</MaximumSize>
                <Children>

              <Overview>
                <ObjectName>TrackOverview</ObjectName>
                <Group>[Channel1]</Group>
                <MinimumSize>1024,72</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>1024,72</MaximumSize>
                <Connection>
                  <ConfigKey>[Channel1],playposition</ConfigKey>
                </Connection>
                <BgColor>#0c0c0c</BgColor>
                <SignalHighColor>#ff5544</SignalHighColor>
                <SignalMidColor>#88ff00</SignalMidColor>
                <SignalLowColor>#2266ff</SignalLowColor>
                <PlayPosColor>#ffffff</PlayPosColor>
                <EndOfTrackColor>#ff2200</EndOfTrackColor>
                <AxesColor>#333333</AxesColor>
                <Type>RGB</Type>
                <!-- Hotcues (auto), cue point, and active loop on the minimap -->
                <DefaultMark>
                  <Align>bottom|right</Align>
                  <Color>#88ff00</Color>
                  <TextColor>#0c0c0c</TextColor>
                  <Text> %1 </Text>
                </DefaultMark>
                <Mark>
                  <Control>cue_point</Control>
                  <Text>C</Text>
                  <Align>top|right</Align>
                  <Color>#ff0033</Color>
                  <TextColor>#ffffff</TextColor>
                </Mark>
                <MarkRange>
                  <StartControl>loop_start_position</StartControl>
                  <EndControl>loop_end_position</EndControl>
                  <EnabledControl>loop_enabled</EnabledControl>
                  <Color>#ff8800</Color>
                  <Opacity>0.75</Opacity>
                  <DisabledColor>#b35f00</DisabledColor>
                  <DisabledOpacity>0.4</DisabledOpacity>
                </MarkRange>
              </Overview>

                  <!-- Rate-adjusted BPM: what is actually playing, not the
                       file's stored tempo. Number, not NumberBpm: the latter
                       is deprecated and parses as a plain Number with no
                       implicit binding, so without the Connection it renders
                       nothing at all. (No double hyphens in an XML comment;
                       they are illegal and reject the whole file.) -->
                </Children>
              </WidgetGroup>

              <!-- Dead strip for the bezel. The panel's bottom edge sits under
                   a lip, so anything drawn in the last few pixels cannot be
                   seen; better empty than half a waveform behind plastic. -->
              <WidgetGroup>
                <ObjectName>BevelPad</ObjectName>
                <MinimumSize>1024,14</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>1024,14</MaximumSize>
                <Children/>
              </WidgetGroup>

            </Children>
          </WidgetGroup>

          <!-- =========== MIDDLE: LIBRARY VIEW (shown when toggled) =========== -->
          <WidgetGroup>
            <ObjectName>LibraryView</ObjectName>
            <!-- Vertical, not horizontal: the two panes live in a nested row so
                 that LibraryBottomPad can sit UNDER both of them. -->
            <Layout>vertical</Layout>
            <!-- The whole panel: the header is hidden while this is up. -->
            <MinimumSize>1024,600</MinimumSize>
            <MaximumSize>1024,600</MaximumSize>
            <Connection>
              <ConfigKey persist="true">[Master],show_library</ConfigKey>
              <BindProperty>visible</BindProperty>
            </Connection>
            <Children>
              <!-- The whole library, as one widget. It owns its own
                   breadcrumb, its own lists and its own bezel strip, because
                   the levels want different row heights and a skin cannot
                   express a menu stack. -->
              <DeckBrowser>
                <ObjectName>DeckBrowser</ObjectName>
                <MinimumSize>1024,600</MinimumSize>
                <MaximumSize>1024,600</MaximumSize>
                <SizePolicy>e,e</SizePolicy>
              </DeckBrowser>
            </Children>
          </WidgetGroup>

          <!-- No bottom button bar. Everything that used to live under the
               waveform (LIBRARY/DECK, the tempo-range pads, LOOP div-2 / x-2)
               is on the hardware, and POWER moved into the header. -->

        </Children>
      </WidgetGroup>
    </Children>
  </WidgetGroup>
  </Children>
</skin>
